    "q-127956": {
      "dom_id": "step-q127956",
      "name": "Question 15",
      "problem": "Find an equation of the plane that passes through the point $Q(4,-2,3)$ and is  perpendicular to the line \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-1+2t \\\\ y=3-2t \\\\ z=5+t.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Note that the position vector of $Q$ is $q=⟨4,-2,3⟩.$ Converting the parametric equations of the line to vector form, we get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-1+2t \\\\ y=3-2t \\\\ z=5+t\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,⟨x,y,z⟩=⟨-1,3,5⟩+t\\text{ }{{⟨2,-2,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{v}.\n$$\n\n   \n\nThe direction vector of the line is $v=⟨2,-2,1⟩.$ So, a normal  vector to the plane is \n\n$$\nn=v=⟨2,-2,1⟩.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-q)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-q\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =q\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨2,-2,1⟩ & =⟨4,-2,3⟩\\cdot ⟨2,-2,1⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨2,-2,1⟩ & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
